 ## Overview
-This method implements the geometry-intensive portion of volume ray casting using dedicated hardware units to perform perspective calculations, gradient estimation, and volume data resampling [S1]. It solves the bottleneck of geometry operations, which consume approximately 80% of computation time in software-based implementations, by generating ray starting points and sample values at high throughput (26–36 million transformed locations per second) [S1]. It is distinguished from sibling methods like [Hardware-Accelerated Volume Rendering via 3D Texturing](2799-hardware-accelerated-volume-rendering-via-3d-texturing.md) by its explicit use of precalculated weight memories and dedicated gradient hardware rather than general-purpose texture sampling units [S1].
+This method accelerates the geometry-intensive portion of direct volume ray casting by offloading perspective calculations, gradient estimation, and volume data resampling to dedicated hardware units [S1]. It solves the bottleneck where software implementations spend up to 80% of computation time on these specific operations [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on general-purpose fragment shaders or 3D texture units, this approach uses specialized address generation and precalculated weight memories to achieve high throughput (26–36 million transformed locations per second) while maintaining 16-bit precision for density and gradient values [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs**
-*   **Input:** 3D volume dataset (voxel values), ray parameters (start point, direction vector per scanline), density lookup table (LUT).
-*   **Output:** Resampled density values and estimated X/Y gradient components for each sampling location along the ray.
+The method operates within the VIRIM architecture, where a farm of parallel geometry units processes scanlines [S1, S2]. Each geometry unit handles the transformation of volume data from object space to the resampled space required for ray casting.
 
-**Data Structures**
-*   **Weight Memory:** Size $256\text{K} \times 16$ bits. Stores precalculated interpolation weights. Addressed by three 6-bit fractional parts (one per coordinate: $u, v, w$) of the resampling location [S1].
-*   **Gradient Buffer:** Limited to $512^3$ resolution, constraining the maximal resampled dataset size [S1].
-*   **Voxel Data:** 16-bit density values.
-*   **Cache:** 1-entry cache for previously used data voxels [S1].
